from full_a import C
LONG = {}

LONG["jeremiah"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Jeremiah was", """Jeremiah was a prophet in Judah for over forty years, from about 627 BC until after Jerusalem fell in 586 BC. He was the son of Hilkiah, a priest from the village of Anathoth, just a few miles north of Jerusalem. He served under the last five kings of Judah. They were years of terrible danger: the mighty Assyrian empire was collapsing, Babylon was rising, and Judah was caught between great powers.

He is often called “the weeping prophet.” His message was hard, his life was lonely, and he was rejected by almost everyone, yet he kept speaking God’s word.""", ("Jeremiah", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """Jeremiah’s story shows what it costs to tell the truth when no one wants to hear it, and how God keeps people going. It also contains some of the Bible’s greatest promises of hope.""")],
cards=[
C("“I am only a youth”", "God called Jeremiah while he was still young, and told him He had chosen him before he was born.",
"""God told Jeremiah, “Before I formed you in the womb I knew you, and before you were born I consecrated you; I appointed you a prophet to the nations.” Jeremiah, probably in his teens or early twenties, answered, “Ah, Lord God! Behold, I do not know how to speak, for I am only a youth.”

God’s reply was direct: “Do not say, ‘I am only a youth’; for to all to whom I send you, you shall go, and whatever I command you, you shall speak. Do not be afraid of them, for I am with you to deliver you.” God touched his mouth and said, “Behold, I have put my words in your mouth.” The job was to “pluck up and break down, to destroy and to overthrow, to build and to plant.” In other words, hard truth first, hope after.

God also warned him that the people would fight against him, “but they shall not prevail against you.”""", ("Jeremiah", 1)),
C("The Temple sermon", "Jeremiah stood at the Temple gate and told the people that their religion was empty while they lived unjustly.",
"""The people of Judah felt safe. They had the Temple of the Lord in Jerusalem and thought God would never let it be destroyed. Jeremiah stood at the Temple gate and told them, “Do not trust in these deceptive words: ‘This is the temple of the Lord, the temple of the Lord, the temple of the Lord.’”

He told them that if they truly changed their ways, if they stopped oppressing foreigners, orphans and widows, stopped shedding innocent blood and stopped following other gods, God would let them stay. But they were stealing, murdering, swearing falsely and then coming to the Temple saying, “We are delivered!” Jeremiah asked, “Has this house, which is called by my name, become a den of robbers in your eyes?” Jesus later quoted this very phrase when He cleared the Temple.

The priests and prophets seized Jeremiah and said he must die. The officials stopped them.""", ("Jeremiah", 7)),
C("The burned scroll", "King Jehoiakim burned Jeremiah’s scroll piece by piece. God told Jeremiah to write it again.",
"""Jeremiah could not go to the Temple, so he dictated all of God’s words to his friend and secretary Baruch, who wrote them on a scroll. Baruch read the scroll aloud at the Temple on a day of fasting. The officials took it to King Jehoiakim. It was winter, and the king sat by a fire in his winter house. As each section was read, he cut it off with a knife and threw it into the fire, until the whole scroll was burned. He ordered Jeremiah and Baruch arrested, but “the Lord hid them.”

God told Jeremiah to take another scroll and write the same words again, adding many more. The king could burn the paper. He could not burn the word of God.""", ("Jeremiah", 36)),
C("Rescued from the well", "Officials threw Jeremiah into a muddy cistern. An Ethiopian official, Ebed-melech, spoke to the king and pulled him out.",
"""By the time Babylon besieged Jerusalem, in 588 BC, Jeremiah told the people that resisting would only lead to death, and that surrender was the way to live. The officials said he was weakening the soldiers’ morale and had him thrown into a cistern, an empty well with mud in the bottom. He sank into the mud.

A court official named Ebed-melech, an Ethiopian, went to the king, King Zedekiah, and said what they had done was evil. The king allowed him to take thirty men and rescue Jeremiah. Ebed-melech was careful. He took old rags and worn clothes, let them down on ropes so that the ropes would not cut into Jeremiah’s arms, and pulled him up. Later God sent Jeremiah a word of promise to Ebed-melech: because he trusted God, he would be spared when the city fell.""", ("Jeremiah", 38)),
C("A letter to the exiles", "Jeremiah wrote to the Jews already in Babylon: build houses, plant gardens, and pray for the city, because God has a plan for you.",
"""Some of the people of Judah had already been taken to Babylon. Prophets there were telling them the exile would be over in two years. Jeremiah wrote them a letter that said the opposite: “Build houses and live in them; plant gardens and eat their produce. Take wives and have sons and daughters... Seek the welfare of the city where I have sent you into exile, and pray to the Lord on its behalf, for in its welfare you will find your welfare.” The exile would last seventy years.

Then comes one of the most loved verses in the Bible: “For I know the plans I have for you, declares the Lord, plans for welfare and not for evil, to give you a future and a hope. Then you will call upon me and come and pray to me, and I will hear you. You will seek me and find me, when you seek me with all your heart.” That promise was given to people in exile, not in comfort.""", ("Jeremiah", 29)),
C("A field and a new covenant", "While Jerusalem was under siege, Jeremiah bought a field as a sign that the people would return. He also promised a new covenant.",
"""With the Babylonian army outside the city and Jeremiah locked in the palace guard’s courtyard, God told him to buy a field in his hometown of Anathoth from his cousin. It was a strange order. The land was in enemy hands. Jeremiah weighed out seventeen shekels of silver, signed the deed in front of witnesses and put it in a clay jar to last many days. It was a sign: “Houses and fields and vineyards shall again be bought in this land.”

Jeremiah also gave a promise that points forward to Jesus: “Behold, the days are coming, declares the Lord, when I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel... I will put my law within them, and I will write it on their hearts. And I will be their God, and they shall be my people... For I will forgive their iniquity, and I will remember their sin no more.” At the Last Supper, Jesus said, “This cup that is poured out for you is the new covenant in my blood.”""", ("Jeremiah", 31)),
C("The fall of Jerusalem", "Jerusalem fell in 586 BC. Jeremiah stayed with the people left behind, and was taken against his will to Egypt.",
"""In 586 BC, after a long siege, Babylon broke through the walls, burned the Temple, the palace and the houses, and took most of the people to Babylon. The Babylonian commander released Jeremiah and let him choose where to go. He chose to stay with the poor who were left in the land.

A few months later, the governor appointed by Babylon was murdered, and the remaining leaders, afraid of revenge, decided to flee to Egypt. Jeremiah warned them that God said to stay. They did not listen, and they took him and Baruch with them to Egypt. The Bible does not record how Jeremiah died. Jewish tradition says he died there in Egypt. A book of mourning called Lamentations, traditionally credited to him, grieves the fall of the city, and in the middle of it comes this line: “The steadfast love of the Lord never ceases; his mercies never come to an end; they are new every morning; great is your faithfulness.”""", ("Lamentations", 3))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Jeremiah was not popular and was not successful in any way people measure. He was ignored, mocked, beaten and jailed. Yet his words have lasted 2,600 years, and the things he said would come true did. Faithfulness matters more than being listened to.

He also shows that you can be honest with God. In chapters often called his “confessions,” Jeremiah complained and argued and said how much he hurt. God did not tell him to be quiet. He told him to keep going.

And the hope in his book is for people who are in a hard season. The promise of a future and a hope was given to exiles, who felt far from home. God says that even then He has a plan, and He will be found by anyone who seeks Him with all their heart.""", ("Jeremiah", 29))])

LONG["jonah"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Jonah was", """Jonah was a prophet from Gath-hepher, a village in Galilee, about 780 BC. The Bible mentions him in the book of 2 Kings, where he told King Jeroboam II of Israel that his kingdom would recover its borders. That was good news for Israel, so Jonah was a popular prophet.

Then God gave him a very different assignment: to go to Nineveh, the capital of Assyria, and speak to the people there. Assyria was Israel’s enemy, known for violent conquest and cruelty. About sixty years later, Assyria destroyed Israel.""", ("2 Kings", 14)),
("Why his story matters", """The book of Jonah is a short story with a big lesson. It is the story of a prophet who ran from God’s call, and of the surprising mercy of God. Jesus Himself pointed to Jonah as a sign.""")],
cards=[
C("Running the wrong way", "God told Jonah to go east to Nineveh. Jonah went to Joppa and boarded a ship for Tarshish, far to the west.",
"""“Arise, go to Nineveh, that great city, and call out against it, for their evil has come up before me.” But Jonah “rose to flee to Tarshish from the presence of the Lord.” Tarshish was probably in southern Spain, about as far in the opposite direction as he could go. He went down to the port of Joppa, found a ship, paid the fare and went below deck to sleep.

We learn later why he ran. It was not because he was afraid of the Ninevites. It was because he was afraid God would forgive them. Jonah did not want Assyria to be forgiven. He wanted them to be punished.""", ("Jonah", 1)),
C("The storm", "God sent a great wind. The sailors cast lots and Jonah was chosen. He told them to throw him overboard.",
"""“But the Lord hurled a great wind upon the sea, and there was a mighty tempest on the sea, so that the ship threatened to break up.” The sailors were afraid. Each cried out to his own god and threw the cargo overboard to lighten the ship. Meanwhile Jonah was fast asleep in the hold.

The captain woke him: “How can you be sleeping? Arise, call out to your god!” The sailors cast lots to find out whose fault it was, and the lot fell on Jonah. They asked him who he was. “I am a Hebrew, and I fear the Lord, the God of heaven, who made the sea and the dry land.” The sailors were terrified. “What shall we do to you, that the sea may quiet down for us?” “Pick me up and hurl me into the sea; then the sea will quiet down for you, for I know it is because of me that this great tempest has come upon you.”

The sailors tried hard to row back to land, but could not. They prayed to the Lord and threw Jonah in. The sea stopped raging at once. The men “feared the Lord exceedingly and offered a sacrifice to the Lord.” Even the pagan sailors showed more respect for God than Jonah had.""", ("Jonah", 1)),
C("Inside the great fish", "A great fish swallowed Jonah. For three days and nights he prayed, and then the fish spat him onto dry land.",
"""“And the Lord appointed a great fish to swallow up Jonah. And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights.” The Bible says “great fish” and does not say what kind. From inside, Jonah prayed: “I called out to the Lord, out of my distress, and he answered me; out of the belly of Sheol I cried, and you heard my voice... The waters closed in over me to take my life; the deep surrounded me; weeds were wrapped about my head.”

He ended his prayer with, “Salvation belongs to the Lord!” Then “the Lord spoke to the fish, and it vomited Jonah out upon the dry land.”

Jesus later used this story. When some religious leaders demanded a sign, He said, “For just as Jonah was three days and three nights in the belly of the great fish, so will the Son of Man be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth.” Jonah’s rescue pointed ahead to Jesus’ resurrection.""", ("Jonah", 2)),
C("Nineveh repents", "Jonah walked through Nineveh announcing that it would be overthrown in forty days. The whole city turned to God.",
"""God told Jonah a second time: “Arise, go to Nineveh, that great city, and call out against it the message that I tell you.” This time he went. Nineveh was so large that it took three days to walk across. On the first day Jonah began to preach: “Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown!”

The result was far beyond what he expected. “And the people of Nineveh believed God. They called for a fast and put on sackcloth, from the greatest of them to the least.” When the news reached the king, he got up from his throne, took off his royal robe, covered himself with sackcloth and sat in ashes. He announced that no person or animal should eat or drink, that everyone must call out to God and turn from violence. “Who knows? God may turn and relent and turn from his fierce anger, so that we may not perish.”

“When God saw what they did, how they turned from their evil way, God relented of the disaster that he had said he would do to them, and he did not do it.”""", ("Jonah", 3)),
C("An angry prophet", "Jonah was furious that God forgave Nineveh. God used a plant to teach him about compassion.",
"""“But it displeased Jonah exceedingly, and he was angry.” He prayed: “O Lord, is not this what I said when I was yet in my country? That is why I made haste to flee to Tarshish; for I knew that you are a gracious God and merciful, slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love, and relenting from disaster. Therefore now, O Lord, please take my life from me, for it is better for me to die than to live.”

God asked, “Do you do well to be angry?” Jonah went out of the city and made a shelter to see what would happen. God made a plant grow up over him to give shade, and Jonah was very glad. The next morning God sent a worm that killed the plant, and a hot east wind blew. Jonah was faint and angry again, wanting to die. God said, “You pity the plant, for which you did not labor, nor did you make it grow... And should not I pity Nineveh, that great city, in which there are more than 120,000 persons who do not know their right hand from their left, and also much cattle?”

The book ends on that question. It never says what Jonah answered.""", ("Jonah", 4))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Jonah’s story is mostly about a prophet who did not want God to be merciful to his enemies. It confronts us: who are the people we would rather see punished than forgiven? God’s love is bigger than our dislike, and He asks us to care for the people He cares about.

It also shows us that we cannot run from God. Jonah went down to Joppa, down into the ship, down into the sea, and down into the fish. But even at the lowest point, God heard his prayer. Wherever we are, the Bible says, God is near.

And the story points to Jesus. Jonah was three days in the fish, and Jesus three days in the tomb. Jonah preached and a pagan city repented. Jesus said, “The people of Nineveh will rise up at the judgment... for they repented at the preaching of Jonah, and behold, something greater than Jonah is here.”""", ("Matthew", 12))])

LONG["daniel"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Daniel was", """Daniel was a young Jewish nobleman from Jerusalem who was taken as a captive to Babylon in 605 BC, when he was probably around sixteen. He lived in Babylon for about seventy years and served under several kings, first Babylonian and later Persian. His name means “God is my judge.”

He was trained for three years in the language and writings of the Babylonians and was given a new name, Belteshazzar. But he stayed loyal to the God of Israel, and God gave him a special ability to understand visions and dreams.""", ("Daniel", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """Daniel’s life shows that you can serve in a foreign culture, even a powerful and hostile one, and still stay true to God. His book includes some of the most famous stories in the Bible and some of its most mysterious visions of the future.""")],
cards=[
C("A test of loyalty", "Daniel and his friends asked not to eat the king’s food, and God blessed them for it.",
"""Daniel and three friends, Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah, were chosen for the king’s service. They were to be fed from the king’s own table. “But Daniel resolved that he would not defile himself with the king’s food, or with the wine that he drank.” He asked the chief of the court officials for permission. The official was afraid he would be punished if they looked worse than the others.

Daniel suggested a ten-day test: give them only vegetables and water, then compare. After ten days they looked healthier and better nourished than all the young men who ate the king’s food. The guard let them keep eating that way.

God gave them knowledge and skill. In every matter of wisdom the king asked, he found them ten times better than all his magicians and enchanters.""", ("Daniel", 1)),
C("The king’s dream", "Nebuchadnezzar had a dream no one could explain. Daniel told him what it was and what it meant.",
"""Nebuchadnezzar had a troubling dream and demanded his wise men tell him what it was and what it meant. They said it was impossible. He ordered them all to be killed, including Daniel and his friends. Daniel asked for time, and the four friends prayed. That night, God revealed the dream to Daniel in a vision.

Daniel went to the king and made clear that the wisdom was from God, not himself. The dream was a huge statue with a head of gold, chest of silver, belly of bronze, legs of iron, feet partly iron and partly clay. A stone, cut without human hands, struck the feet, and the whole statue crumbled to dust. The stone became a great mountain that filled the earth. Daniel explained that the statue stood for a series of kingdoms, and the stone for a kingdom God would set up that would never be destroyed. Nebuchadnezzar fell on his face: “Truly, your God is God of gods and Lord of kings.” He made Daniel ruler over the province of Babylon.""", ("Daniel", 2)),
C("Writing on the wall", "At a feast, a hand wrote words on the wall. Daniel read them to King Belshazzar, and that night Babylon fell.",
"""Many years later, in 539 BC, King Belshazzar held a huge feast for a thousand nobles. He ordered that the gold and silver cups taken from God’s Temple in Jerusalem be brought, and they drank from them, praising their idols. Suddenly the fingers of a human hand appeared and wrote on the plaster wall. The king’s face turned pale, and his knees knocked together.

No one could read it. The queen mother remembered Daniel. Now an old man, he was brought in. He refused the king’s offer of rewards and told him, “You have lifted up yourself against the Lord of heaven.” The words were Mene, Mene, Tekel, Parsin. Daniel explained: God had numbered the days of his kingdom and brought it to an end; the king had been weighed and found wanting; his kingdom would be divided and given to the Medes and Persians. “That very night Belshazzar the Chaldean king was killed.” Babylon fell to the Persian army.""", ("Daniel", 5)),
C("The lions’ den", "Jealous officials tricked King Darius into a law that forbade prayer. Daniel kept praying and was thrown to the lions.",
"""Under the new Persian rule, Daniel did so well that King Darius planned to put him over the whole kingdom. The other officials were jealous. They looked for something to accuse him of and found nothing. They concluded, “We shall not find any ground for complaint against this Daniel unless we find it in connection with the law of his God.”

They persuaded the king to sign a law that for thirty days anyone who prayed to any god or man other than the king would be thrown into the lions’ den. Daniel heard that it was signed. “He went to his house where he had windows in his upper chamber open toward Jerusalem. He got down on his knees three times a day and prayed and gave thanks before his God, as he had done previously.” The officials found him praying.

The king was upset, since he liked Daniel, but could not change the law. He told Daniel, “May your God, whom you serve continually, deliver you!” A stone was put over the mouth of the den. The king spent the night fasting and could not sleep.""", ("Daniel", 6)),
C("Shut mouths", "In the morning Daniel was alive. God’s angel had shut the lions’ mouths.",
"""At first light, the king hurried to the den. In a troubled voice he called, “Daniel, servant of the living God, has your God, whom you serve continually, been able to deliver you from the lions?” Daniel answered, “O king, live forever! My God sent his angel and shut the lions’ mouths, and they have not harmed me, because I was found blameless before him; and also before you, O king, I have done no harm.”

The king was overjoyed and had Daniel lifted out. No kind of harm was found on him “because he had trusted in his God.” The men who had accused him were thrown into the den with their families, and the lions overpowered them before they hit the floor.

Darius then sent a message to the whole empire: “For he is the living God, enduring forever; his kingdom shall never be destroyed, and his dominion shall be to the end. He delivers and rescues; he works signs and wonders in heaven and on earth.”""", ("Daniel", 6)),
C("Visions of the future", "Daniel prayed for his people, and God gave him visions of future kingdoms and of the coming of God’s Kingdom.",
"""Daniel lived to be very old, serving in Persia until at least 536 BC. He read the book of Jeremiah and realized the seventy years of exile were almost over. He prayed a long prayer of confession for himself and the whole nation: “To you, O Lord, belongs righteousness, but to us open shame.” Even as he prayed, the angel Gabriel arrived with an answer.

Daniel’s book also records visions of great beasts representing empires, and one of the most famous passages in the Bible: “I saw in the night visions, and behold, with the clouds of heaven there came one like a son of man, and he came to the Ancient of Days... And to him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom, that all peoples, nations, and languages should serve him; his dominion is an everlasting dominion, which shall not pass away.” Jesus often called Himself the “Son of Man,” and when He was on trial, He said that they would see “the Son of Man seated at the right hand of Power and coming on the clouds of heaven.”

At the end, God told Daniel, “Go your way till the end. And you shall rest and shall stand in your allotment at the end of the days.”""", ("Daniel", 7))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Daniel lived in a culture that did not share his faith, and he did not hide it. But he was also respectful, hard-working and honest. Even his enemies could find nothing wrong with him except his devotion to God. That is a good goal.

His prayer life was steady. He prayed three times a day, with the window open, before the lions’ den and after. His courage came from habit. When the pressure came, he did not have to decide whether to pray. He was already praying.

And Daniel’s book gives hope for the long view. Kingdoms of gold, silver, bronze and iron rise and fall. Kings are proud and then humbled. But God’s kingdom has no end. When the news looks bad, Daniel reminds us who holds history.""", ("Daniel", 6))])

LONG["ruth"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Ruth was", """Ruth was a young woman from Moab, a country east of the Dead Sea. The Moabites were Israel’s neighbors and often their enemies, and they worshiped a god named Chemosh. Ruth lived about 1100 BC, in the time of the judges, a time of trouble in Israel.

Her story starts with an Israelite family from Bethlehem, which means “house of bread.” There was a famine in Bethlehem, so a man named Elimelech took his wife Naomi and two sons, Mahlon and Chilion, to live in Moab. Ruth’s story begins with tragedy.""", ("Ruth", 1)),
("Why her story matters", """The book of Ruth is a short love story and also a story of loyalty, kindness and God’s care in ordinary life. Ruth, an outsider, became the great-grandmother of King David and an ancestor of Jesus.""")],
cards=[
C("Three widows", "Naomi’s husband and both sons died in Moab. She decided to return to Bethlehem, and Ruth went with her.",
"""In Moab, Elimelech died. Naomi’s two sons married Moabite women, Orpah and Ruth. After about ten years, both sons died too. Naomi was left with two daughters-in-law and no one else. She was far from home with no income, no children and no husband. When she heard that God had provided food in Bethlehem, she decided to go back.

She urged her daughters-in-law to return to their own families. “The Lord grant that you may find rest, each of you in the house of her husband!” Orpah kissed her and went back. But Ruth would not leave. Her answer is one of the most famous in the Bible: “Do not urge me to leave you or to return from following you. For where you go I will go, and where you lodge I will lodge. Your people shall be my people, and your God my God. Where you die I will die, and there will I be buried. May the Lord do so to me and more also if anything but death parts me from you.”

Naomi saw that Ruth was determined, and said no more.""", ("Ruth", 1)),
C("Gleaning in the field", "In Bethlehem, Ruth went to pick up leftover grain behind the harvesters. She ended up in the field of Boaz.",
"""They arrived in Bethlehem at the beginning of the barley harvest. The whole town was stirred. “Is this Naomi?” the women asked. She said, “Do not call me Naomi (pleasant); call me Mara (bitter), for the Almighty has dealt very bitterly with me.”

God’s law told farmers to leave the edges of their fields and any fallen grain for the poor, the foreigner and the widow. Ruth went out to glean, which meant walking behind the harvesters picking up leftover stalks. It was hard, humbling work. “As it happened, she came to the part of the field belonging to Boaz,” a relative of Naomi’s late husband. The Bible says “as it happened,” but the story makes clear that God was leading her.

Boaz, a wealthy and respected man, arrived from town and greeted his workers, “The Lord be with you!” “The Lord bless you.” He noticed Ruth and asked who she was.""", ("Ruth", 2)),
C("Boaz’s kindness", "Boaz told Ruth to stay in his field, told his workers to protect her, and gave her extra grain and food.",
"""The foreman told Boaz she was the Moabite who came back with Naomi and had been working hard since morning. Boaz went to her: “Now, listen, my daughter, do not go to glean in another field or leave this one, but keep close to my young women. Let your eyes be on the field that they are reaping, and go after them. Have I not charged the young men not to touch you? And when you are thirsty, go to the vessels and drink what the young men have drawn.”

Ruth bowed to the ground and asked, “Why have I found favor in your eyes, that you should take notice of me, since I am a foreigner?” Boaz answered that he had heard what she had done for her mother-in-law: “The Lord repay you for what you have done, and a full reward be given you by the Lord, the God of Israel, under whose wings you have come to take refuge!”

At mealtime he invited her to eat with them, gave her roasted grain until she was full and had some left over, and secretly told his workers to pull out stalks for her to find. She came home with about an ephah of barley, nearly a bushel, and Naomi said, “Blessed be the man who took notice of you!” It turned out Boaz was a close relative, one of the family’s “redeemers.”""", ("Ruth", 2)),
C("At the threshing floor", "Naomi sent Ruth to Boaz at night. He promised to do what a kinsman-redeemer should.",
"""A “kinsman-redeemer” was a close relative who could buy back a family’s land and marry a widow to carry on the family name. Naomi told Ruth to wash, put on perfume and her best clothes, and go to the threshing floor at night where Boaz was sleeping after the harvest feast. She was to lie down at his feet and ask him to be her redeemer.

In the night, Boaz woke up and found a woman at his feet. “Who are you?” “I am Ruth, your servant. Spread your wings over your servant, for you are a redeemer.” Boaz was kind and respectful. “May you be blessed by the Lord, my daughter. You have made this last kindness greater than the first in that you have not gone after young men, whether poor or rich. And now, my daughter, do not fear. I will do for you all that you ask, for all my fellow townsmen know that you are a worthy woman.” But there was a complication: another relative was closer than he was. Boaz sent her home before dawn with six measures of barley, so no one would gossip.""", ("Ruth", 3)),
C("The wedding", "Boaz went to the town gate. The closer relative declined, and Boaz bought the family land and married Ruth.",
"""Boaz went up to the town gate, where business was done, and sat down with ten elders. When the closer relative came by, Boaz told him that Naomi was selling her late husband’s land, and gave him first chance. “I will redeem it.” Then Boaz added that he would also have to marry Ruth the Moabite, to raise up an heir for her dead husband. The man said, “I cannot redeem it, lest I impair my own inheritance.” He took off his sandal to seal the deal.

Boaz announced to the elders, “You are witnesses this day that I have bought from the hand of Naomi all that belonged to Elimelech... Also Ruth the Moabite, the widow of Mahlon, I have bought to be my wife.” The elders and the people said, “May the Lord make the woman, who is coming into your house, like Rachel and Leah, who together built up the house of Israel.”

Boaz and Ruth married, and they had a son. The women said to Naomi: “Blessed be the Lord, who has not left you this day without a redeemer... He shall be to you a restorer of life and a nourisher of your old age, for your daughter-in-law who loves you, who is more to you than seven sons, has given birth to him.” Naomi took the baby and held him on her lap.""", ("Ruth", 4)),
C("A king’s grandmother", "The baby was named Obed. He became the grandfather of King David, and Ruth is listed in the family of Jesus.",
"""The neighbor women named the baby Obed. He became the father of Jesse, and Jesse was the father of David. The book ends with the family line from Perez to David. That list shows that Ruth, a Moabite foreigner, became the great-grandmother of Israel’s greatest king.

The story continues to the New Testament. In Matthew’s Gospel, the family tree of Jesus includes “Boaz the father of Obed by Ruth.” Only five women are mentioned in that genealogy, and Ruth is one of them. The family of the Messiah included a widow from a foreign land, a woman who chose loyalty and faith.""", ("Matthew", 1))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Ruth’s story shows how God works in ordinary events. There are no miracles in the book, no angels or fire from heaven. There are famine, grief, a field, a man’s kindness and a decision to be loyal. Yet in all of it, God was bringing about something big.

Ruth’s love was costly and practical. She left her home to care for an older woman who could give her nothing. Real love often looks like that: staying when it would be easier to go.

And Boaz is a picture of Jesus. He saw a poor foreigner, welcomed her, provided for her and paid the price to make her part of his family. The Bible says that Jesus is our Redeemer, who took notice of us when we had nothing and brought us into His family.""", ("Ruth", 4))])

LONG["aaron"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Aaron was", """Aaron was the older brother of Moses and Miriam. He was a Levite, born in Egypt, three years before Moses. While Moses was raised in Pharaoh’s palace and spent forty years in the desert, Aaron stayed with their people, who were slaves. When Moses returned at eighty, Aaron was eighty-three.

Aaron became Moses’ spokesman, and then Israel’s first high priest. His family, the priests, served at the tabernacle, and later at the Temple, for about 1,500 years.""", ("Exodus", 4)),
("Why his story matters", """Aaron’s life shows what it is to serve alongside a more famous brother, and how a good man can have a serious failure. He also points forward to Jesus, the final High Priest.""")],
cards=[
C("Moses’ spokesman", "Moses said he could not speak well, so God gave him Aaron to speak for him.",
"""When God called Moses at the burning bush to lead Israel out of Egypt, Moses protested: “Oh, my Lord, I am not eloquent... I am slow of speech and of tongue.” God said, “Is there not Aaron, your brother, the Levite? I know that he can speak well. And behold, he is coming out to meet you, and when he sees you he will be glad in his heart.”

Aaron met Moses at the mountain of God and they embraced. Together they went to the elders of Israel, and Aaron told them everything God had said. They went to Pharaoh together. When Pharaoh demanded a miracle, Aaron threw down his staff and it became a serpent. Pharaoh’s magicians did the same with their staffs, but Aaron’s serpent swallowed theirs. For the plagues that followed, Aaron often stretched out the staff over the Nile and the land. It was clearly a shared mission, though Moses was the leader.""", ("Exodus", 7)),
C("The first high priest", "God chose Aaron and his sons as priests and gave him special clothing.",
"""At Mount Sinai, God gave Moses detailed instructions for the tabernacle and the priesthood. Aaron was to be high priest, and his sons, Nadab, Abihu, Eleazar and Ithamar, were to be priests. They were consecrated with a seven-day ceremony of washing, anointing with oil and sacrifices.

Aaron’s clothing was beautiful and full of meaning: a blue robe with golden bells and pomegranates on the hem, a linen tunic, and a breastpiece with twelve precious stones, one for each tribe of Israel. “Aaron shall bear the names of the sons of Israel in the breastpiece of judgment on his heart, when he goes into the Holy Place.” On his turban was a golden plate engraved “Holy to the Lord.” Once a year, on the Day of Atonement, he alone could enter the Most Holy Place with blood to make atonement for the people’s sins.""", ("Exodus", 28)),
C("The golden calf", "While Moses was on the mountain, the people asked Aaron to make them a god. He made a golden calf.",
"""Moses spent forty days on Mount Sinai. The people got impatient and went to Aaron: “Up, make us gods who shall go before us. As for this Moses, the man who brought us up out of the land of Egypt, we do not know what has become of him.” Aaron told them to bring their gold earrings. He melted them and shaped a golden calf. “These are your gods, O Israel, who brought you up out of the land of Egypt!” He built an altar and announced a feast to the Lord. The next day they got up early and offered sacrifices and “rose up to play,” a polite way of saying they partied wildly.

God told Moses what was happening, and Moses came down, breaking the stone tablets in his anger. He confronted Aaron: “What did this people do to you that you have brought such a great sin upon them?” Aaron’s answer is almost funny, and sad: “They gave me the gold, and I threw it into the fire, and out came this calf!” About 3,000 people died that day. Moses prayed for the people, and in the end God did not destroy them. Aaron himself was spared.""", ("Exodus", 32)),
C("Fire from the Lord", "Aaron’s sons Nadab and Abihu offered unauthorized fire and died. Aaron stayed silent.",
"""After the tabernacle was set up, Aaron and his sons began their work. On the first day, fire came from the Lord and burned up the offering, and all the people shouted and fell on their faces. Then two of Aaron’s sons, Nadab and Abihu, took their censers, put fire and incense in them and offered “unauthorized fire before the Lord, which he had not commanded them.” Fire came out and consumed them.

Moses told Aaron what God had said: “Among those who are near me I will be sanctified, and before all the people I will be glorified.” The Bible adds, “And Aaron held his peace.” He lost two sons in a day. He and his family could not even mourn in the usual way since they were in the middle of their service. The story is a hard one and shows that God is holy, and that approaching Him should not be treated lightly.""", ("Leviticus", 10)),
C("The staff that budded", "When some challenged Aaron’s priesthood, God made his staff sprout blossoms and almonds overnight.",
"""Later, a man named Korah and 250 others challenged Moses and Aaron: “You have gone too far! For all in the congregation are holy.” The ground opened and swallowed Korah and his men, and fire consumed the 250. When the people grumbled again and blamed Moses and Aaron, a plague broke out. Aaron ran into the middle of the crowd with his censer of incense and stood “between the dead and the living,” and the plague stopped, but 14,700 had died.

To settle the question of who was chosen, God told Moses to collect twelve staffs, one for each tribe, with Aaron’s name on the one for Levi. They were placed in the tabernacle overnight. In the morning, “behold, the staff of Aaron for the house of Levi had sprouted and put forth buds and produced blossoms, and it bore ripe almonds.” It was placed before the ark as a reminder.""", ("Numbers", 17)),
C("His death on Mount Hor", "Aaron died at age 123 on Mount Hor, and his son Eleazar became high priest.",
"""Near the end of the forty years, Moses and Aaron were at Meribah, where the people complained again about the lack of water. God told them to speak to the rock, but Moses struck it twice in anger. Because they did not trust God enough to honor Him in front of the people, neither Moses nor Aaron would enter the promised land.

God told Moses to take Aaron and his son Eleazar up Mount Hor. There Moses removed Aaron’s priestly garments and put them on Eleazar. “And Aaron died there on the top of the mountain.” He was 123. The whole house of Israel mourned for thirty days.

Aaron’s blessing is still used today: “The Lord bless you and keep you; the Lord make his face to shine upon you and be gracious to you; the Lord lift up his countenance upon you and give you peace.” The letter to the Hebrews says that Jesus is a better and permanent High Priest, who “always lives to make intercession” for us.""", ("Numbers", 20))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Aaron is a reminder that God uses people with real weaknesses. He stood by Moses faithfully for years and also gave in to the crowd at the foot of Sinai. Good leaders can fail, and the Bible does not hide it. Yet God still gave him the priesthood.

His story also shows how serious and how wonderful it is to approach God. The priests needed washing, special clothing and a sacrifice every time. All of that points ahead to Jesus, who made one perfect sacrifice. The letter to the Hebrews tells us, “Let us then with confidence draw near to the throne of grace.”

And Aaron’s blessing is a good prayer for anyone: that the Lord bless you and keep you, and give you peace.""", ("Numbers", 6))])

LONG["barnabas"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Barnabas was", """Barnabas was an early Christian leader, around AD 30 to 60. His real name was Joseph, and he came from the island of Cyprus. He was a Levite, from the tribe of priests. The apostles gave him the nickname Barnabas, which means “son of encouragement.” That nickname says a great deal about who he was.

He was a man of generosity, faith and courage. The Bible says he was “a good man, full of the Holy Spirit and of faith.”""", ("Acts", 4)),
("Why his story matters", """Barnabas is the man who believed in people others gave up on. He was a bridge for Paul, for John Mark and for the church in Antioch. His story shows how much one encourager can change.""")],
cards=[
C("A generous gift", "Barnabas sold a field and laid the money at the apostles’ feet to help the poor believers.",
"""In the first months after Pentecost, thousands of people in Jerusalem believed. Many had traveled from afar for the festival and stayed. The believers shared what they had so that no one was in need. “Joseph, who was also called by the apostles Barnabas, a Levite, a native of Cyprus, sold a field that belonged to him and brought the money and laid it at the apostles’ feet.”

That one verse introduces him. Rather than keep his wealth, he put it to use for others. Right after, the Bible tells of Ananias and Sapphira, who pretended to give everything but kept some back. The contrast was clear: Barnabas gave honestly and freely.""", ("Acts", 4)),
C("Vouching for Saul", "When Paul, then called Saul, came to Jerusalem, the disciples were afraid of him. Barnabas took him to the apostles.",
"""Saul of Tarsus had been the church’s worst persecutor. After he met Jesus on the road to Damascus, he began preaching in Damascus and then had to escape over the city wall in a basket. When he got to Jerusalem, “he attempted to join the disciples. And they were all afraid of him, for they did not believe that he was a disciple.”

“But Barnabas took him and brought him to the apostles and declared to them how on the road he had seen the Lord, who spoke to him, and how at Damascus he had preached boldly in the name of Jesus.” It was a risk. If Saul was lying, it could have cost lives. Barnabas trusted what he saw, and because of his word, Saul was welcomed.""", ("Acts", 9)),
C("The church in Antioch", "The apostles sent Barnabas to Antioch, where many non-Jews were believing. He went to find Saul to help him.",
"""Believers who fled the persecution after Stephen’s death had spread out, and some in Antioch in Syria began telling Greeks, non-Jews, about Jesus. A large number believed. When news reached Jerusalem, the church sent Barnabas to see what was going on. “When he came and saw the grace of God, he was glad, and he exhorted them all to remain faithful to the Lord with steadfast purpose, for he was a good man, full of the Holy Spirit and of faith. And a great many people were added to the Lord.”

The work was growing too large for one person, so Barnabas went to Tarsus, about 100 miles away, to find Saul and bring him to Antioch. For a whole year they taught together. It was in Antioch that the disciples were first called Christians. When a famine hit, the church there collected money, and Barnabas and Saul carried it to Jerusalem.""", ("Acts", 11)),
C("The first mission", "The church at Antioch sent out Barnabas and Saul, with John Mark, on the first missionary journey.",
"""While the leaders at Antioch were worshiping and fasting, the Holy Spirit said, “Set apart for me Barnabas and Saul for the work to which I have called them.” They prayed, laid hands on them and sent them off. Barnabas is named first, since at that time he was the senior leader. They took Barnabas’ younger cousin, John Mark, as an assistant.

They sailed to Cyprus, Barnabas’ home island, then to what is now Turkey. Gradually Saul, now called Paul, began to take the lead, and the Bible starts saying “Paul and his companions.” In the town of Lystra, after Paul healed a lame man, the crowd decided Barnabas was Zeus and Paul was Hermes, and brought oxen to sacrifice. Barnabas and Paul tore their clothes and rushed out: “Men, why are you doing these things? We also are men, of like nature with you, and we bring you good news.”""", ("Acts", 13)),
C("A disagreement over Mark", "Paul and Barnabas disagreed so sharply about John Mark that they separated. Barnabas took Mark with him.",
"""Some time later, Paul suggested they go back and visit the churches. Barnabas wanted to take John Mark again. But Mark had left them in the middle of the first journey and gone home. Paul did not want to take him. “And there arose a sharp disagreement, so that they separated from each other. Barnabas took Mark with him and sailed away to Cyprus, but Paul chose Silas and set out.”

It was not a happy ending, but Barnabas stuck to his character. He gave Mark a second chance. Years later, Paul wrote from prison, “Get Mark and bring him with you, for he is very useful to me for ministry.” Mark became a trusted helper of both Paul and Peter, and according to early tradition he wrote the Gospel of Mark. Without Barnabas’ encouragement, it may never have happened.

The Bible does not say more about Barnabas’ later life. Paul spoke of him warmly in his letters, as a fellow worker who, like him, supported himself with his own labor.""", ("Acts", 15))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Barnabas was never the star. He introduced Paul to the apostles, then brought Paul to Antioch, then stepped aside as Paul took the lead. He gave Mark a second chance when Paul would not. The kingdom of God has room for people whose job is to lift others up.

His example is practical. Encouragement is not just saying nice things. It is being generous with money, with your reputation and with your time. Barnabas put his own name on the line for Saul and Mark.

Whom could you encourage this week? A new believer, a person who failed, someone who is overlooked. The Bible says, “Therefore encourage one another and build one another up.” Sometimes the most important people in a story are the ones who gave someone else a chance.""", ("Acts", 11))])
